WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.840
I think I had a point of maybe maturation in my being an artist where I realized for

00:08.840 --> 00:15.760
me it's about the activist work, specifically it has to fit with the other kind of work

00:15.760 --> 00:17.120
I'm already would be doing.

00:17.120 --> 00:22.280
I want to make something that I could totally make a poster of it.

00:22.280 --> 00:26.880
I don't know if that makes sense, but I want to make things that are very in line with

00:26.880 --> 00:28.480
what I already do.

00:28.480 --> 00:37.920
So I ended up mostly concentrating on illustration, but then this opportunity to do this came up.

00:37.920 --> 00:45.600
It's like this like Trans Recovery Home in West Philly called Morris Home.

00:45.600 --> 00:52.080
They got an opportunity to have mural arts and I think other programs in the city fund

00:52.080 --> 00:57.760
them to get two different large murals made and put up somewhere in the city.

00:57.760 --> 01:02.080
So I got asked to do the second one.

01:02.080 --> 01:03.240
So that's what this is.

01:03.240 --> 01:09.000
This is like similar to the process with the performance statistics billboard.

01:09.000 --> 01:15.880
It's pictures of folks that we're living in the house at the time and also words from

01:15.880 --> 01:18.440
people that we're living in the house at the time.

01:18.440 --> 01:27.360
I just took the images from this photo shoot that we did in this park one day.

01:27.360 --> 01:30.400
This person named Angel Edwards took the pictures.

01:30.400 --> 01:36.800
So I took the pictures and then I did a workshop where I asked them, what do you want people

01:36.800 --> 01:37.800
to know about Morris Home?

01:37.800 --> 01:39.760
What do you want people to know about you?

01:39.760 --> 01:43.840
What is it really important that they get out of the mural and they gave me a lot of

01:43.840 --> 01:46.080
words, very direct.

01:46.080 --> 01:53.680
Then I just chose the ones I thought made sense for a mural and I put them up.

01:53.680 --> 01:57.960
It was really intense.

01:57.960 --> 02:00.080
Someone passed out working on it.

02:00.080 --> 02:01.080
It was very hard.

02:01.080 --> 02:05.080
It's probably the hardest thing project wise I've ever done.

02:05.080 --> 02:09.800
But it is pretty awesome.

02:09.800 --> 02:12.720
I think it's just so much of this stuff.

02:12.720 --> 02:16.760
I started working in 2020 so I just know the internet and I'm like, oh, all the radical

02:16.760 --> 02:26.520
ideas are like, they're like in my, they're like something during a reading I did at school,

02:26.520 --> 02:32.800
which has meaning and value or they're like online which also has meaning and value.

02:32.800 --> 02:42.800
But like I feel like the public space is like totally like right when you kind of, not

02:42.800 --> 02:46.400
my kind of values, like not the values of my friends.

02:46.400 --> 02:51.800
I don't feel like there's like a big mural of an Andre Lureford quote, maybe some

02:51.800 --> 02:57.440
are deep in Oakland but like not every, not all over it's not common, you know.

02:57.440 --> 03:06.400
So I thought it was like really cool to be able to like try to make something that is

03:06.400 --> 03:16.480
like closer to like how me and like friends who are queer like, or who are leftist, like

03:16.480 --> 03:22.000
other things like take up a lot of space and like have a lot of resources to make like

03:22.000 --> 03:25.720
the long lasting peace about these ideas.

03:25.720 --> 03:30.040
I will say like it's something I'm experimenting with because like the more permanent it is,

03:30.040 --> 03:35.480
I feel like the more like you have to be.

03:35.480 --> 03:40.440
Like I think it would be hard to have a mural of like the broken cop car with like flowers

03:40.440 --> 03:41.440
coming out.

03:41.440 --> 03:47.720
I do know somebody who does work like that like Jessica Sabagall in California is a mural

03:47.720 --> 03:54.200
is two does do like some pretty rad like mural work, but I think for the most part it is,

03:54.200 --> 03:59.920
it feels like it's easier in illustrations to like go all the way with it like what I

03:59.920 --> 04:10.800
want to say, but I think with this, I tried to like be, yeah it was it was just cool to

04:10.800 --> 04:16.600
be able to say like we are joyful and we feel rage and like try to make something that

04:16.600 --> 04:22.440
was like warm and beautiful but also like honest try to be honest in that way.

04:22.440 --> 04:29.920
And yeah, so that was like one experiment in like trying to see if I could say like something

04:29.920 --> 04:36.480
that I really believe also on a wall, but in like a publicly funded thing where like people

04:36.480 --> 04:37.880
have to look at this every day.

04:37.880 --> 04:43.200
So I have to think about like what is like the best way to put that in a sentence.

04:43.200 --> 04:49.240
I did that and I was like no I haven't done a mural sentence like not a painted one and

04:49.240 --> 04:58.800
I feel like best part is yeah, it's just cool to get support and like funding and resources

04:58.800 --> 05:01.120
for like some of these ideas.

05:01.120 --> 05:02.280
That's cool.

05:02.280 --> 05:08.160
And I think one person who was an assistant, Devon Downs was like standing with me looking

05:08.160 --> 05:09.160
at the wall.

05:09.160 --> 05:13.080
I think on a day when I was feeling kind of down, it was like imagine what this is going

05:13.080 --> 05:15.960
to look like in the fall in the winter.

05:15.960 --> 05:20.040
Like some kid is going to grow up and they're just going to have this in their neighborhood

05:20.040 --> 05:25.160
and like that vision of like it just being a part of the landscape in a really cool way

05:25.160 --> 05:27.200
is exciting.

05:27.200 --> 05:32.600
Um, but yeah, I would say hardest parts were just like the physical pain.

05:32.600 --> 05:40.240
Like there was like I have to before you install them, you have to like prime the wall,

05:40.240 --> 05:44.400
like you prime a wall in your house or something with like these really long poles.

05:44.400 --> 05:49.640
So because I kept bending backwards like one time my back hurts so bad I lay down and

05:49.640 --> 05:56.000
then I couldn't like get out of bed and like it's just like a very physically intense process,

05:56.000 --> 06:00.600
very heavy buckets of like materials getting pulled over the place like I had to get training

06:00.600 --> 06:04.280
to work like the heavy machinery like work a lift.

06:04.280 --> 06:09.200
It's like very very very physically hard, hot like it's 95 degrees, 90 degrees, we're

06:09.200 --> 06:12.680
like working like it's very tough.

06:12.680 --> 06:16.480
The process of making is very physically tough.

06:16.480 --> 06:17.720
That's hard.

06:17.720 --> 06:23.840
And also it's just hard like the process of making art like that, you know, like in my

06:23.840 --> 06:31.160
life I live in a neighborhood, I live in a micro neighborhood that's very gay and my social

06:31.160 --> 06:34.320
circles are very gay or gay adjacent.

06:34.320 --> 06:41.520
And so I kind of bought in my life from place to place where I'm going to a space where

06:41.520 --> 06:46.200
I'm going to be fine or like I'm transiting through places where I don't know if I will

06:46.200 --> 06:53.240
be but like I just I don't know I don't get I'm not bothered on like transit.

06:53.240 --> 06:59.440
But this was like different because even though that neighborhood is pretty gay like yeah

06:59.440 --> 07:03.560
it's just different to be putting up art about trans people like in public and I think

07:03.560 --> 07:10.640
I was very like this, you know, I'm already kind of like an introverted person and then

07:10.640 --> 07:15.440
it was hard for me to hold the shape of the muralist like because when you're at least

07:15.440 --> 07:20.120
advice that I got from Sammy who helped me paint it and is like way more experienced

07:20.120 --> 07:21.120
muralist than me.

07:21.120 --> 07:25.680
Sammy called not like she was like you know you want to be open and friendly like you're

07:25.680 --> 07:31.520
making this new thing and like people don't know you like you have to be friendly open

07:31.520 --> 07:34.840
to people in the neighborhood coming up and asking questions and I think because of

07:34.840 --> 07:40.440
the topic I was very like closed but really most people were supportive pretty much up

07:40.440 --> 07:44.440
until one of the last days when somebody with like a dog came and like was like I don't

07:44.440 --> 07:49.560
want to see this like these people are ugly this is like a mental illness and I feel like

07:49.560 --> 07:54.240
he like almost confirmed like the fear that I had been feeling like the whole time.

07:54.240 --> 07:58.840
So I think that was something that was hard for me in the process but also kind of made

07:58.840 --> 08:04.920
me think because I'm like still figuring out how I want to like be in the world as an

08:04.920 --> 08:10.960
artist with these ideas and I think it was different to have to be accountable in public

08:10.960 --> 08:12.600
to like what the public thinks about it.

